Websites Should Be Printable
2026-10-24
I read a lot of printed papers these days. While I enjoy reading on a laptop as much as the next guy, marking up a printed document engages me better. Also, there's something about sitting with a whiteboard and a printed document that makes me rightly feel like I'm doing something important. So I've been thinking about how to best allow others to enjoy this medium for content that I write.
Luckily for me, printed documents were a major focus of web design a while ago. You can print any webpage you like, by hitting CTRL-P and hoping your printer driver decides to cooperate today. I tested this on my own webpage, foolishly thinking I would have a decent print job without any effort.
It wasn't great, although you can at least see all the content. The text was too big by about 2 levels, the header looks awful when printed, and my links look awfully gray in black-and-white. Totally unacceptable.
Making Things Printable
Edit 2026-10-26: I feel it's relevant to say, this has almost entirely changed already because of feedback I received from people both on lobste.rs and HN about obvious things I'd missed. I'm currently simplifying my stylesheet as much as I can. Thanks!
Luckily, the web community has had this aforementioned focus on printing documents from HTML pages. CSS has selectors to allow styling specifically printed content, and they can be useful. Here's my print-related snippet for this page now:
.onlyPrint {
  display: none;
}

@media print {
  .noPrint {
    display: none;
  }

  .onlyPrint {
    display: inline;
  }

  p,
  ol {
    font-size: 12pt;
  }

  h3, h4 {
    font-size: 13pt;
  }

  h2 {
    font-size: 14pt;
  }

  h1 {
    font-size: 16pt;
  }

  .giallo {
    border: 3px solid black;
  }

  .giallo-l {
    font-size: 11pt;
  }

  a {
    color: black !important;
  }
}
You can see the effects of this snippet by hitting CTRL-P or the print button on this page. Here's the functions this does for me, in order:
onlyPrint can be added as a class to allow elements which only show up in the printed document. Right now, that means tagging a small author attribution to the subtitle.
noPrint is used to remove something from the printed website. I put it on the header, because it looks honestly very ugly in printed format.
Adjusting font sizes for print is very important. The variable-scale text which I use on my website looks awful on a printed page. 12pt is a fairly well accepted size, so I figured it'll do. A slightly larger size for the headers makes sense as well.
The giallo tweaks are for codeblocks. Honestly, codeblocks are still the ugliest part even after some tweaking. They want to come out as one chunk and not break over page borders, and if anyone has a solution to that I'd be greatly appreciative.
Blue links, as much as I like them on the site, look awful in grayscale. Turning them the same color as other text is fine. Feel free to pull any code you like from the CSS, I'm not protective of it. I would personally love to experience more webpages that think about how printing works.
Why You Should Do This
After attempting to optimize my website in this way, I am sympathetic for those who wouldn't prioritize printing. Hiding and showing all the right things in print took me a decent chunk of my morning. My site isn't very complex. I also doubt how many people regularly print webpages out. I might be the only one.
Still, if all your website consists of is text, allowing people to experience the text in their favorite medium is never a bad thing. The digital medium allows a lot of flexibility. The road to good software is paved with kindnesses from software engineers to their users. One day, perhaps someone shall find this kindness and smile, even if just for a fleeting moment. And that will be worth it.
Edit 2026-10-26: I was informed by a friend that this post ended up on the front page of HN and lobste.rs after I submitted it. Fun! Despite the several flaws I am now informed of in my CSS, I hope this article at least pushes a few to consider this medium more carefully. Thanks all for reading!

Why I still teach OpenGL ES 3.0
Jan 27, 2026
Little C++ OpenGL ES 3.0 sample compiled to the web with emscripten
Introduction
It’s been a few years that I have been giving this Introduction to Computer Graphics module (a trimester) at SAE Institute Geneva and I have always been wondering if and when I would need to transition my course from OpenGL ES 3.0 to more modern API.
Back in November, I was at the Graphics Programming Conference (my blog post here), and this subject reemerged for me during the conversations with people from the industry as well as from the talks. From the Teardown talk (all the conference’s talks are available on the website), the original game worked in OpenGL 3.3 and from the X-Plane talk, the rendering engine was using OpenGL 2.1. But both those talks were about how they transitioned to more modern API to have better graphics. This sounds like the sunset of OpenGL.
Two talks really caught my attention about education for Computer Graphics: the talk Bridging Pixels and Code: Teaching Computer Graphics to Technical Artists from Matthieu Delaere that focus on students creating their own rasterizer from scratch and the talk from Mike Shah that focuses on the next steps after the tutorial. The latter talk actually made me think about if the actual idea to do the transition to modern API for my course made still sense.
What have to be taken into account for a transition is that all the courses need to be translated to this new API/framework and updated depending on the new concept added. So if there is a transition to be made, it needs to be really worth it compare to OpenGL ES 3.0.
The course content
The goal of the introduction in computer graphics module is for the students to implement a 3d scene with a graphics API. What is important to understand is that they should not implement a generic 3d renderer, but specifically a 3d scene, so the final executable is not supposed to be data-driven. For example, it would be completely ok to have such implementation:
void DrawScene(const Pipeline& pipeline, const Camera& camera)
{
  //Cull the scene from the camera POV

  //Draw each 3d object by hand

}
Since when I started to give this module, I have been following the learnopengl.com structure but using OpenGL ES 3.0 instead. Each chapter is like a list of samples that have to be implemented and tested (Hello Triangle, Hello Texture, etc…)
But why specifically this version of OpenGL? OpenGL ES 3.0 is a mobile graphics API, released in August 2012, so about 14 years ago (which is a LONG time in computer graphics). I chose it because it is the most cross-platform version of OpenGL in my opinion, with this version you can run the same C++ program (with some #ifdef) and the same shaders for:
Windows: while I encountered some driver issues throughout the year, OpenGL ES 3.0 is compatible with OpenGL 4.3, so I just assume OpenGL 4.3 on Windows not to worry.
Linux: some of my workstations boot on Linux by default, and it is good practice to compile the same C++ code with another compiler.
Android: this one is a bit complicated as the actual android app boots from Java and calls the native lib containing the code.
Nintendo Switch: we have two devkits at school and except removing glew or glad and replacing it with the embedding OpenGL wrapper, it works fine.
WebGL2: my favorite reason of using OpenGL ES 3.0 is that it is compatible with WebGL2 with emscripten (except float color attachment that is an extension…), which allows my students to port their samples to their own website/blog. It is the case with this blog post where I simply put the demo out of emscripten on the web page.
iOS or MacOSX: unfortunately not supported anymore, but none of my students work on a Mac as this is a Games Programming bachelor.
The same demo but running on the Switch
Compare to more modern API, synchronization is taken care of by the driver in OpenGL. This allows to skip most of the setup of a lot of boilerplate initialization. This is nice, but is paid with the cost of the driver overhead added for this.
However, one of the hassles of the first course is having the students’ laptop chooses the wrong GPU on laptop (using the Intel integrated GPU instead of the Nvidia GPU). Last year, you just needed to set the whole Nvidia configuration to be on “Performant” mode, but with a new Windows update, you need to set individually the application that is to be performant in the Windows settings, not the Nvidia settings anymore. Also, there is this trick for Windows:
#ifdef __cplusplus
extern "C" {
#endif

__declspec(dllexport) DWORD NvOptimusEnablement = 1;
__declspec(dllexport) int AmdPowerXpressRequestHighPerformance = 1;

#ifdef __cplusplus
}
#endif
And this (supposedely) enables the discrete GPU and not the integrated one (I am happy this is a solved problem in modern API like Vulkan). Let’s see how we tackle other subjects!
